RStudio has an online version we can use for developing on the cloud. Cloud environments are frequently used when we do not have enough resources or suitable devices to use R / RStudio. These environments allow us to configure the environment and change computing resources so we can run our code in a remote machine designed for performing these tasks, without investing in hardware. Normally the use of these services require a subscription fee but we can use it free with some limitations.

# First time using Posit Cloud

For using Posit cloud go to their website [here](https://posit.cloud/), and create your own account with a free subscription.

![](posit_cloud_logiin.png){fig-align="center" width="512"}

Once your account has been successfully created you will see the Posit Cloud environment tab. Here you can go to the **Your** **Workspace** section and create a **New Project** of RStudio.

![](workspace_layout.png){fig-align="center" width="516"}

Once the project has been created you will see an RStudio page with the latest R version available.

![](project.png){fig-align="center" width="502"}

The RStudio page your are seeing works similar to the local RStudio IDE you have on your computer. The only difference you need to know is that you are working on a cloud environment so you don't have access to all files and source of your local computer. Every script, rmarkdown or file you create is located on the cloud.

\pagebreak

# Working with files on Posit Cloud

If you need to add data files or upload your own scripts on the RStudio Cloud project you are working with, you can do it with the **Upload** option available on the Files browser.

![](import_01.png){fig-align="center" width="359"}

Select the import button and then choose the file you want to upload.

![](import_02.png){fig-align="center" width="332"}

In the other way you can download the scripts you are working with and save them on your local computer. For doing that just select the file and then on the `...` available on the file browser select Export.

![](export_01.png){fig-align="center" width="387"}

You can change the name and then download it.

![](export_02.png){fig-align="center" width="383"}

